<h2 id="facts">Facts this plan relies on</h2>
<p>Read from the pinned tools in this repo (firebase-tools <strong>15.22.3</strong>, firebase-functions <strong>7.4.0</strong>) or from Google's docs on Oct 1, and checked by both round-1 reviewers. "Verify" means a phase step proves it before anything depends on it.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Fact</th><th>So this plan…</th></tr>
<tr><td>J1</td><td><code>functions:list</code> shows a scheduled v2 function only as <code>scheduleTrigger: {}</code> plus the label <code>deployment-scheduled: "true"</code> (<code>gcp/cloudfunctionsv2.js:299-305</code>; labels are copied through). Schedule, time zone, retry settings, deadline and target are not visible to the CLI.</td><td>The Scheduler job is read by Christie and checked by the script (V2). The guard can't prove the schedule; it proves her reading matches (V2).</td></tr>
<tr><td>J2</td><td>The job is <code>projects/my-clay-hub/locations/us-central1/jobs/firebase-schedule-&lt;id&gt;-us-central1</code>, keeping the function id's case (<code>firebase-schedule-vaultExport-us-central1</code>; the Cloud Run service is lower-cased, <code>vaultexport</code>). It's an HTTP <code>POST</code> to the function's <code>uri</code>, with an OIDC token signed as <strong>the function's own runtime account</strong> and no audience set (<code>gcp/cloudscheduler.js:118-166</code>). The default v2 time zone is UTC.</td><td>V2's reading; fixtures keep the case.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J3</td><td>For a scheduled function the CLI <strong>forces</strong> the Cloud Run service-level <code>run.invoker</code> binding to exactly <code>[runtime account]</code>, on create and update (<code>release/fabricator.js:382-389, 484-492</code>).</td><td>The declaration has no invoker; the expected one is derived (V3).</td></tr>
<tr><td>J4</td><td><code>attemptDeadline</code> = <code>max(min(timeoutSeconds, 1800), 180)</code> s; a schedule's timeout is capped at 1,800 s (<code>validate.js:28-29, 152</code>). firebase-functions 7.4.0 has no <code>attemptDeadline</code> option.</td><td><code>timeoutSeconds: 1800</code> → 30-min deadline; the wait budget is 1,500 s (V7).</td></tr>
<tr><td>J5</td><td>The job is only patched when schedule, time zone, deadline or retry changed; retry is compared with a <strong>partial</strong> match, so live retry fields not in the code are neither cleared nor noticed (<code>cloudscheduler.js:82-108</code>). The job is created/patched <strong>after</strong> the function (<code>fabricator.js:161, 182</code>): a failed job upsert leaves a live function with no (or an old) job and a non-zero exit. Removing a schedule from code orphans the job: it keeps POSTing.</td><td>V3 requires all five retry values; V2 reads the whole job; the runbook covers each partial state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J6</td><td>onSchedule pre-fills <code>timeZone</code> and all five retry keys with a reset marker that serialises to <code>null</code> (<code>runtime/manifest.js:32-45, 113-135</code>), so an unset field is <em>present</em> as <code>null</code>. <code>requiredAPIs</code> is a list of <code>{api, reason}</code> objects: <code>[{api: "cloudscheduler.googleapis.com", reason: …}]</code>. The handler's <code>event.scheduleTime</code> is the <code>X-CloudScheduler-ScheduleTime</code> header, or now if absent; a thrown error is logged with <code>logger.error</code> and answered 500; the wrapper writes the response itself (<code>v2/providers/scheduler.js:42-66</code>).</td><td>V3 checks numbers and the string <code>"UTC"</code>, not presence; O6.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J7</td><td>Caller: Google's Firestore page names only roles (Owner / Datastore Owner / Import Export Admin). <code>datastore.databases.export</code>, <code>datastore.operations.get</code>, <code>datastore.operations.list</code> are real permissions (Import Export Admin also holds <code>…databases.import</code>, which overwrites production). <em>Inference, not a doc statement:</em> the caller needs no bucket permission to export, because the Firestore service agent writes the files.</td><td>V4; the first real export proves it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J8</td><td>The Firestore service agent needs an explicit grant on a bucket in another project. <strong>Datastore-mode</strong> docs list the narrow export set: <code>storage.buckets.get</code>, <code>storage.objects.create</code>, <code>storage.objects.delete</code>, <code>storage.objects.list</code> (and <code>buckets.get</code> + <code>objects.get</code> for import). Native-mode/Firebase docs say Storage Admin. The agent is created on demand; its address is shown on Firestore's Import/Export page.</td><td>V4: the narrow set, proven by a real export; Storage Admin on that one bucket as the written fallback.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J9</td><td><code>nam5</code> is a US multi-region (us-central1 + us-central2, witness us-east1). Datastore docs say the bucket should be in the <strong>same location</strong>; Firestore docs say "near". The matching GCS location is <code>US</code>; <code>us-central1</code> is narrower than the database.</td><td>V10: <code>US</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J10</td><td>Native API: <code>exportDocuments</code> takes a caller-chosen <code>outputUriPrefix</code>; the operation can be polled (<code>done</code>, <code>error</code>, <code>metadata.operationState</code>, <code>metadata.outputUriPrefix</code>). <code>snapshotTime</code> must be in the past, on a whole minute, inside the version-retention window (1 h without PITR, 7 days with). The LRO exists server-side before the call returns.<br>Datastore docs: a prefix can't be reused; operations are listed "for a few days" after completion; the list has no documented filter on the prefix.<br>A complete export holds <code>&lt;folder&gt;/&lt;folder&gt;.overall_export_metadata</code> (file name = folder name).</td><td>V7, V8.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J11</td><td>Scheduler: <code>retryCount</code> 0–5 — retried that many times "or until the next scheduled execution, whichever first"; <code>maxRetryDuration</code> 0 = unlimited, and when both are set it retries until <em>both</em> limits are reached. A late response is cancelled as <code>DEADLINE_EXCEEDED</code>; whether the Cloud Run request keeps running is undocumented.</td><td>V6 (<code>maxRetrySeconds: 0</code>), V7 (own wait budget).</td></tr>
<tr><td>J12</td><td>Cloud Monitoring's "metric absent" condition is capped at 23.5 h; a threshold condition may use a window up to 25 h. Uptime checks support <strong>service-agent authentication</strong> to Cloud Run (the token's audience is the configured URL); public checks run from at least three regions; default timeout 10 s.</td><td>V9.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J13</td><td>Soft delete defaults to 7 days; a listing excludes soft-deleted objects. Lifecycle age counts from creation; deletion of a folder's objects is asynchronous over up to 24 h. A retention lock is irreversible, and the export agent needs <code>objects.delete</code>.</td><td>V11, V12, V14.</td></tr>
<tr><td>J14</td><td>Firestore allows up to 50 concurrent exports per project: Google won't stop a duplicate.</td><td>V7 makes a duplicate harmless rather than impossible (O5).</td></tr>
</table>

<h2 id="choices">Choices made here (Christie can overrule any)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>#</th><th>Choice</th><th>Why</th></tr>
<tr><td>V1</td><td><strong>A new codebase, <code>vault</code></strong> (<code>functions/vault/</code>), separate from <code>core</code> (the canary). Own <code>firebase.json</code> entry, lockfile, tests at <code>tests/functions/vault/</code>, attempt receipts <code>deployed/my-clay-hub/functions-vault/…</code>. Direct, exact-pinned dependencies: firebase-functions 7.4.0, firebase-admin 14.5.0, <code>@google-cloud/firestore</code> and <code>@google-cloud/storage</code> (versions = those firebase-admin 14.5.0 already resolves).</td><td>A canary redeploy never touches the backup, and vice versa. Note: <code>iam-expectations.json</code> is shared, so from V-3 on every <code>core</code> attestation also lists the vault accounts' roles.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V2</td><td><strong>The Scheduler read-back is Christie's Console reading, checked by the script.</strong> <code>--attest</code> evidence gains two keys, <strong>always present</strong>: <code>schedulerJobList</code> (every job name in <code>us-central1</code>, a complete list) and <code>schedulerJobs</code> (per scheduled function: name, state, schedule, timeZone, targetUri, httpMethod, oidcServiceAccount, oidcAudience, body, headers, attemptDeadline and the five retry values). For <code>core</code> both are empty. The script requires: the list equals exactly the declared scheduled functions' job names; each job ENABLED; every field equal to <code>declarations.json</code> (J4's deadline computed from the manifest's timeout); <code>targetUri</code> = the live <code>uri</code>; POST; OIDC account = the runtime account; audience absent or = the live <code>uri</code>; no body; no custom headers.<br>
  New record field <code>scheduler_attested</code> = yes / no / <code>n/a</code> (no scheduled function in the codebase). <strong>A Scheduler reading is always needed</strong> for a deploy of a codebase that has a schedule. <code>deploy_verified</code> is unchanged; <code>--status</code> shows all three.<br>
  <strong>Limit, stated plainly:</strong> the job exists before Christie can read it, and the reading proves what the Console showed at that moment, not later drift. The guard never proves the schedule runs; the 8-day freshness alert (V9) is what catches a job that doesn't.</td><td>J1: the CLI can't read the job; no gcloud. Same model as M3.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V3</td><td><strong>The guard's rules for a schedule</strong> (F13 amended for schedules only):
  <ul>
  <li>a declaration's <strong>key set is exact</strong>: HTTP <code>{trigger: "https", serviceAccount, invoker, ingress}</code>; schedule <code>{trigger: "schedule", serviceAccount, ingress, schedule: {schedule, timeZone, retryCount, maxRetrySeconds, minBackoffSeconds, maxBackoffSeconds, maxDoublings}}</code>. Any unknown or missing key refuses by name.</li>
  <li>each endpoint has exactly one trigger, and it matches its declaration: <code>httpsTrigger</code> (as now) or <code>scheduleTrigger</code>; every other trigger kind is still refused by name;</li>
  <li><code>scheduleTrigger</code>'s <code>schedule</code> and <code>timeZone</code> are strings and its five retry values are <strong>numbers</strong>, each equal to the declaration (J6: <code>null</code> means "unset" and refuses); <code>timeZone</code> must be <code>"UTC"</code>;</li>
  <li><code>[.requiredAPIs[].api]</code> is exactly <code>["cloudscheduler.googleapis.com"]</code> with each entry's keys exactly <code>{api, reason}</code> when the codebase has a schedule; empty otherwise;</li>
  <li>a schedule's expected <code>run.invoker</code> (for <code>--attest</code>) is exactly <code>serviceAccount:&lt;its runtime account&gt;</code> (J3); <code>timeoutSeconds</code> ≤ 1800; the other F14 rules unchanged.</li>
  </ul>
  F8 accepts <code>scheduleTrigger: {}</code> with label <code>deployment-scheduled: "true"</code> only for a declared schedule, and an HTTP function only without that label. The unauthenticated probe still runs on every <code>uri</code> and must be refused. The backstop (<code>predeploy-check.sh:221-234</code>) applies the same branches. Because the schedule lives in <code>declarations.json</code> (an <code>ATTEST_FILES</code> member), every effective schedule change shows in <code>--diff</code>.</td><td>M6, J5, J6. Params, secrets and every other trigger stay refused. If a scheduled function were ever public, the probe would answer 2xx (caught as "answered") but would also start a real export — public is refused before upload for that reason too.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V4</td><td><strong>Identities, each minimal</strong> (role ids exactly as written; Christie transcribes the id, not the display title):
  <ul>
  <li><code>vault-export@my-clay-hub.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code>: custom project role <code>projects/my-clay-hub/roles/vaultExporter</code> = <code>datastore.databases.export</code>, <code>datastore.operations.get</code>, <code>datastore.operations.list</code>; and <code>projects/tinker-hq-vault/roles/vaultLister</code> on the vault bucket. It can start and read exports and list file names; it can't read or write a document, read an export's contents, or change anything in the vault.</li>
  <li>The Firestore service agent: <code>projects/tinker-hq-vault/roles/firestoreExportWriter</code> (the four J8 permissions) on the vault bucket only, granted in V-4 (bootstrap).</li>
  <li><code>vault-watch@my-clay-hub.iam.gserviceaccount.com</code>: <code>vaultLister</code> on the vault bucket only. Its code only lists (reading <code>timeCreated</code> from the listing; never <code>exists()</code> or <code>getMetadata()</code>, which need <code>buckets.get</code>).</li>
  </ul>
  Admin clients are built with explicit project and database names (no project discovery). <code>actAs</code> comes from Christie's Owner role (M7). Any widening needs the exact denied permission from a real error and Christie's approval.</td><td>Parent D-4. The two <code>tinker-hq-vault</code> grants can't be read by <code>--attest</code> (it reads only <code>my-clay-hub</code>'s IAM); V-1/V-4 screenshots plus V-4/V-5 behaviour are their record.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V5</td><td><strong>Schedule:</strong> <code>every sunday 09:00</code>, <code>timeZone: "UTC"</code> (02:00/03:00 Denver; UTC has no DST gap).</td><td>Parent D-4.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V6</td><td><strong><code>vaultExport</code> limits:</strong> <code>retryCount: 3</code>, <code>maxRetrySeconds: 0</code>, <code>minBackoffSeconds: 600</code>, <code>maxBackoffSeconds: 3600</code>, <code>maxDoublings: 2</code> → retries about 10, 20 and 40 min after a failure, then stop (J11; observed in V-4). <code>timeoutSeconds: 1800</code>, <code>maxInstances: 1</code>, <code>concurrency: 1</code>, <code>cpu: 1</code>, <code>memory: 512MiB</code>, <code>ingressSettings: ALLOW_ALL</code> (as the canary; Scheduler calls the public URL with a token).</td><td>A failed Sunday run gets three tries in the next hour and a quarter. 512 MiB is cheap headroom for the gRPC admin client.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V7</td><td><strong>Each scheduled run exports once; retries resume; a rare duplicate is a second complete copy, never a mixed folder</strong> (O5). The run key is the <strong>UTC date of <code>event.scheduleTime</code></strong> (<code>D</code>, e.g. <code>2026-10-04</code>); Scheduler's retries carry the same scheduled time (verify in V-4). Each run:
  <ol>
  <li><strong>Done already?</strong> List <code>weekly/D-</code> in the vault bucket (paged; a hard cap, and hitting it throws). A folder is complete when it holds <code>&lt;folder&gt;.overall_export_metadata</code>. Any complete folder → log "already done" and succeed.</li>
  <li><strong>Running already?</strong> List this database's operations (<code>listOperationsAsync</code> on <code>projects/my-clay-hub/databases/(default)</code>, paged, filtered client-side to exports whose prefix starts <code>gs://&lt;bucket&gt;/weekly/D-</code>; a cap, and hitting it throws — never "none"). One not done → wait on it. One done <code>SUCCESSFUL</code> but no complete folder → throw (inconsistent: needs a person).</li>
  <li>Otherwise <strong>start</strong> an export to a fresh prefix <code>weekly/D-&lt;6 random chars&gt;</code> with V8's <code>snapshotTime</code>, and wait on it.</li>
  </ol>
  Waiting polls every 15 s with a <strong>budget of 1,500 s from the start of the run</strong>; on expiry it throws ("still running; the retry resumes"), so the error path and alert always run before Cloud Run's 1,800 s kill. Success needs: <code>done</code>, no <code>error</code>, state <code>SUCCESSFUL</code>, the expected prefix, and the complete folder visible in the bucket. Anything else throws.<br>
  <strong>Duplicates:</strong> two runs racing within seconds, or a lost reply that Google hasn't listed by the next retry, can each start an export. Each goes to its own prefix, so the result is two complete copies. At most one export per invocation, and at most 4 invocations per scheduled run. A Force run on another day gets its own key (an honest extra copy).<br>
  <strong>State</strong> is the bucket (durable for 56 days) plus the operation list (used only for "still running", which is well inside its few-day retention). No Firestore collection, so <code>vault-export@</code> never gets data access.</td><td>Both round-1 reviews: the operation list alone can't be the record, and <code>maxInstances</code> isn't a lock. Christie chose (Oct 1) to allow a rare second copy rather than build a lock.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V8</td><td><strong>Point-in-time:</strong> <code>snapshotTime</code> = the start of the current minute minus one minute.</td><td>A consistent copy across collections; inside the retention window with or without PITR (J10). Dropped if the first real export rejects it (logged).</td></tr>
<tr><td>V9</td><td><strong>The 8-day alert: an uptime check on a small private HTTP function</strong>, <code>vault/vaultFresh</code>. It lists <code>weekly/</code> (paged, capped), takes complete folders only (V7 step 1), and reads the newest metadata object's <code>timeCreated</code>. 200 when that's at most <strong>7 days 18 hours</strong> old; 503 when older or none; 500 on any error. The body holds only the folder name, the age and which case it was (stale / none / error), the triage for an alert. <code>withReached</code>. Options: <code>invoker: [Monitoring service agent]</code> (address read in V-0, never assembled), <code>serviceAccount: vault-watch@</code>, <code>ingressSettings: ALLOW_ALL</code> (checkers come from public IPs), <code>maxInstances: 1</code>, <code>concurrency: 10</code>, <code>timeoutSeconds: 30</code>, <code>cpu: 1</code>, <code>memory: 256MiB</code>.<br>
  The uptime check: HTTPS, URL = exactly the live <code>uri</code> the guard reads back (the token's audience), service-agent authentication, period 15 min, check timeout 30 s (cold starts), 3 US regions. Alert policy: failing for 6 hours → email. So the email arrives at about 8 days.</td><td>J12. Monitoring drives the check, so it doesn't share Cloud Scheduler with the thing it watches: it catches the scheduler never running, the export broken, grants lost, folders deleted, and the watcher itself broken. Christie chose it (O4) and the 7 d 18 h threshold (Oct 1).</td></tr>
<tr><td>V10</td><td><strong>Bucket: location <code>US</code></strong> (J9). Name: <code>my-clay-hub-vault-exports</code>, else <code>my-clay-hub-vault-exports-&lt;4 random chars&gt;</code> (the parent's <code>&lt;PROJECT_ID&gt;-…</code> candidate is the same name here). Recorded before anything uses it. Standard class, uniform access, public access prevention on.</td><td>"Same location" as nam5, and it survives a <code>us-central1</code> outage. O2.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V11</td><td><strong>Retention:</strong> lifecycle delete at age 56 days (about 8 weekly copies); soft delete on at 7 days; no versioning.</td><td>Parent D-4. Prefixes are never reused, so versioning adds nothing.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V12</td><td><strong>No retention lock</strong>, for now. Residual: the Firestore agent (via <code>objects.delete</code>) could delete exports in this one bucket; soft delete recovers them <strong>for 7 days only</strong> — a deletion unnoticed for a week is permanent. The freshness alert notices a deleted newest copy within hours.</td><td>The export needs <code>objects.delete</code>; a lock is irreversible. Revisit after a few months of clean exports.</td></tr>
<tr><td>V13</td><td><strong>Failure alert:</strong> a log-based alert policy in <code>my-clay-hub</code> with filter <code>resource.type="cloud_run_revision" AND resource.labels.service_name="vaultexport" AND severity&gt;=ERROR</code> (including request logs, so a 500 or a killed request also counts), emailing Christie. One email per incident; auto-close after 7 days.</td><td>Parent D-4. No secrets (the guard refuses them).</td></tr>
<tr><td>V14</td><td><strong>Restore runbook</strong> <code>docs/my-clay-hub/DATA-RESTORE.md</code>: choose the newest <strong>complete</strong> folder (its metadata file present and named after it — lifecycle deletes folders gradually, so a folder can be half there); the temporary read grant (<code>storage.buckets.get</code> + <code>storage.objects.get</code>) for the <em>importing</em> database's Firestore service agent, removed afterwards; an import <strong>overwrites matching documents and deletes nothing</strong>, so into a non-empty database it is a merge, not a rollback. The rehearsal happens in Phase E (O3).</td><td>Parent D-4.</td></tr>
</table>

